# BST Problems: Quick Revision Notes

## 1. BST Core Rule

For every node:

```text
All values in the left subtree  < root.val
All values in the right subtree > root.val
```

### Two BST superpowers

```text
1. Value comparison tells us which direction to move.
2. Inorder traversal gives values in sorted order.
```

---

## 2. How to Recognize the Pattern

```text
Search / Insert / Delete
→ Compare the target with root.val

Validate BST
→ Pass an allowed min-max range

Kth smallest / Minimum difference
→ Use inorder because it is sorted

Recover swapped BST
→ Find where inorder order breaks

LCA in BST
→ Check whether both nodes are on the same side

Modify a subtree
→ Reconnect the root returned by recursion
```

---

## 3. Search in BST: LeetCode 700

### Pattern

```text
target == root.val → found
target < root.val  → search left
target > root.val  → search right
```

```java
class Solution {
    public TreeNode searchBST(TreeNode root, int val) {
        if (root == null || root.val == val) {
            return root;
        }

        if (val < root.val) {
            return searchBST(root.left, val);
        }

        return searchBST(root.right, val);
    }
}
```

### Important return rule

```java
return searchBST(root.left, val);
```

The deeper call's answer must travel back through every previous call.

This is wrong:

```java
searchBST(root.left, val); // Result is ignored
return null;
```

---

## 4. Inorder Traversal

```text
Left → Node → Right
```

For a BST, this produces sorted order.

```java
private void inorder(TreeNode root) {
    if (root == null) {
        return;
    }

    inorder(root.left);

    // Process the current node here
    System.out.println(root.val);

    inorder(root.right);
}
```

### Meaning of `prev`

```text
prev = previously processed node in inorder order
curr = node currently being processed
```

`prev` is not necessarily the parent.

