## 9. Delete Node in BST: LeetCode 450

### Pattern

```text
Step 1: Search for the node.
Step 2: Handle its children.
Step 3: Return the updated subtree root.
```

### Three deletion cases

```text
No left child  → return right child
No right child → return left child
Two children   → use successor from right subtree
```

```java
class Solution {
    public TreeNode deleteNode(TreeNode root, int key) {
        if (root == null) {
            return null;
        }

        if (key < root.val) {
            root.left = deleteNode(root.left, key);
        } else if (key > root.val) {
            root.right = deleteNode(root.right, key);
        } else {
            // Case 1: No left child
            if (root.left == null) {
                return root.right;
            }

            // Case 2: No right child
            if (root.right == null) {
                return root.left;
            }

            // Case 3: Two children
            TreeNode successor = findMin(root.right);

            // Copy successor value
            root.val = successor.val;

            // Remove original successor node
            root.right = deleteNode(root.right, successor.val);
        }

        return root;
    }

    private TreeNode findMin(TreeNode root) {
        while (root.left != null) {
            root = root.left;
        }

        return root;
    }
}
```

### Why reconnect the return value?

```java
root.left = deleteNode(root.left, key);
```

Deletion may change the root of the left subtree. The parent must update its pointer to that new root.

